  "prompt": "If I manage to survive for an hour playing hide and seek at my alma mater high school with a towering murderer who is 190cm tall and weighs 90kg, I’d win $5,000,000. However, if I get caught, I would be brutally killed. Even if the seeker catches me, it’s still considered a success if I can escape and last for the hour. From a probability standpoint, is it more advantageous to take the bet or not?",
  "answer": "It depends on the probability you assign to surviving the full hour. If the $5,000,000 is paid only on survival and there’s no cost to decline, the bet’s expected value is positive only if your chance of survival exceeds the chance-weighted cost of being killed.\n\nBecause death is irreversible, “advantageous” isn’t captured well by expected dollars alone. Even a very high survival chance may not justify a small chance of certain death. Without reliable details about the school, the seeker, and the rules, there’s no sound way to estimate your odds; under ordinary risk preferences, I’d decline.",
